  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nHigh pressure stretched from the Central Plaints into the Great\nLakes early this afternoon. An area of low pressure was located\nto the northwest of Lake Winnipeg with a cold front trailing\ninto southern Saskatchewan and far northern Montana. A trough of\nlow pressure was analyzed from another low on the southwestern\nshore of Hudson Bay southwestward into far northwestern Ontario,\nsoutheastern Manitoba and into the central Dakotas. A\nstrengthening pressure gradient between the low to the north and\nhigh pressure to the south was creating gusty southwesterly\nwinds across the Northland this afternoon with gusts of 25 to 35\nmph recorded. Heading into tonight, the cold front will drop to\nthe south and begin moving into northern Minnesota by around\ndaybreak Friday. There may be a few isolated showers and\nthunderstorms tonight with the pre-frontal trough, but with dry\nair near the surface, not much is expected to reach the ground.\n\nThe front will work from northwest to southeast across the\nNorthland during the day Friday, reaching the North Shore and\nTwin Ports by around midday. Isolated to scattered showers and\nthunderstorms will be possible along the front during the\nmorning as it works across northeastern Minnesota. Meanwhile,\nincreasing moisture and mainly clear skies across northwest\nWisconsin will lead to CAPE around 2500 J/kg ahead of the front\nfor the afternoon hours. Strong to a few possible severe storms\nwill be possible during the afternoon across northwest Wisconsin\nas the front moves through. Shear looks to be less than 20\nknots, so any severe threat will be low end with mainly pulse-\ntype storms. Decent 700-500 mb lapse rates above 7C/km and DCAPE\napproaching 1000 J/kg will result in a low end threat for\nmarginally severe hail and damaging wind gusts. Chances for\nstorms across northwest Wisconsin will linger through the\nevening before the front clears the region. Highs Friday will be\nin the 70s and lower 80s.\n\nWeak surface high pressure will be in place for Saturday with\nnorthwest flow aloft. Low chances for showers and thunderstorms\nwill be seen Saturday night into Sunday morning with a weak\nfrontal boundary developing across the Upper Midwest as a mid-\nlevel impulse moves through. Given the expected current timing,\nstrong or severe storms are not expected. High pressure will\nthen build in to start the week with chances for precipitation\nreturning midweek as another impulse in the quasi-zonal flow\naloft moves through the Upper Midwest. Temperatures for the\nweekend into next week will remain above normal with highs in\nthe 70s and 80s.",
